WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.220
- Some contact lens brands say

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:03.740
that it's okay to sleep in them,

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:05.913
but as a general rule, it's really not.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:14.580
My name is Dr. Irina Belinsky.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.970
I am an eye surgeon specializing

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.360
in oculoplastics and ocular oncology.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.930
Contact lenses are safe,

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.960
but they're safe if you take
care of them appropriately,

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.370
and part of good contact lens hygiene

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:29.540
is not sleeping in your contact lenses.

00:00:29.540 --> 00:00:33.380
Think of the contact lens as
foreign material in your eye.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:34.430
It's sort of like a sponge,

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:35.980
and to be comfortable,

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.020
the contact lens needs to be moist,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:41.120
so, it absorbs moisture from your eye,

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:43.250
and it can also trap bacteria,

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.940
and so, it's very important

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:47.250
to take your contact lenses out

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:48.950
to clean them periodically.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:52.670
Leaving them in when you sleep
can cause an eye infection

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:54.390
that can sometimes be really, really bad.

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.770
A lot of the infections can be mild

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:59.950
and go away with antibiotic eye drops,

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.320
but some infections can
be really, really severe,

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:03.620
and even if they go away,

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.130
they can cause scarring

00:01:05.130 --> 00:01:06.410
of the surface of your eye,

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:07.400
which is the cornea,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.030
and that scar can cause

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.330
sort of a permanent change in your vision.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:13.600
Sometimes an infection can be devastating,

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.320
and it can completely cause the eye

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.560
to scar to the point
that someone might need

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.860
a corneal transplant or
lose vision completely.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.230
Really serious blinding kind of infections

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:28.250
related to contact lens
wear are rare, fortunately.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:29.083
If they were common,

00:01:29.083 --> 00:01:30.760
then nobody would ever
wear contact lenses.

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:31.940
So, they are exceedingly rare,

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.940
and they usually have to do with really,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.400
really bad contact lens hygiene.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.790
So, good contact lens hygiene
has to do with a few things.

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:41.890
One, knowing if your contact lens needs

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:44.360
to be changed every
month or every two weeks

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:45.840
and trying to stick to that,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.180
even if you have to put a little reminder

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.080
in your calendar.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:49.890
The more common thing that we see

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:51.730
is just contact lens overwear.

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.510
The cornea gets devoid of oxygen.

00:01:54.510 --> 00:01:58.060
So, a contact lens does
start to break down over time

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:00.470
so it doesn't have a smooth surface.

00:02:00.470 --> 00:02:04.310
It's gonna form
micro-breakdown kind of edges,

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.270
and the contact lens will also dry out.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:09.650
I mean, your eye just
doesn't have enough moisture

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:11.970
to keep a contact lens in there forever.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.100
If you think of the
contact lens as a sponge,

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.540
like the sponge in your kitchen,

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.890
you have to clean it every so often,

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:20.950
and you might even have to
replace it every so often,

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.860
because it just doesn't
do the job right anymore.

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:28.060
A contact lens can become kind of folded

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.630
and entrapped in the
eye underneath the lid,

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:34.530
so, I have seen that

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.160
where patients come in
with chronic red eye,

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:39.640
and you look around very carefully

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.870
and sometimes, and this is also rare,

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:44.250
but you can actually
extract a contact lens

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:45.800
that has probably been there for months.

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.170
It can happen if you put it in wrong.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:50.480
It can happen if you sleep in it.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:52.700
It can happen if you don't take it out,

00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:54.230
or if you forget to take it out.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:55.683
Take your contact lenses out.

00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.250
Don't leave them in.

00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.773
Take them out every day.

